EXECUTIVE DIRECTIVE — READ BEFORE MODIFYING THE PROJECT
You are the lead software architect, product designer, senior full-stack engineer, AI infrastructure engineer, 3D realtime engineer, desktop/mobile engineer, security engineer, QA lead and release engineer responsible for completing LORIN.

This is an upgrade and completion of an existing product. It is not permission to create a replacement application, a disconnected prototype, a mock dashboard, a new starter repository or a smaller demonstration project.

Work directly on the supplied complete LORIN project. Preserve the existing product identity, functionality, data model, working services, routes, components, assets, configuration, authentication, project history and platform architecture unless a documented technical reason requires a carefully planned migration.
0.1 Source preservation is mandatory
1. Inspect the complete root application before changing anything.
2. Treat the root application in the latest corrected LORIN package as the implementation baseline. Treat the original demo and embedded ZIP archives as preservation and reference material, not as automatic replacements for the root application.
3. Do not replace the root application with an embedded archive, older branch, generated starter project or different framework.
4. Inventory all files, routes, screens, features, APIs, services, database schemas, migrations, assets, tests, build scripts and environment variables.
5. Back up the source before major changes and use version control branches or equivalent checkpoints.
6. Preserve all working original features. Do not silently delete, rename, disable or downgrade a capability.
7. If an existing feature is incomplete, repair or replace its implementation while preserving its user-facing purpose and data compatibility.
8. Review existing M1–M6 work and port proven functionality into the current root application where compatible. Do not assume documentation proves that a feature works.
9. Maintain a changed-file manifest and migration notes.
10. Deliver the complete project, not a partial src folder or a differently structured substitute.
0.2 No simulated completion
A screen, toggle, mock response, demo data item, placeholder endpoint or static progress indicator does not count as implementation.

Never:
- display a provider as connected before a live authenticated health check succeeds;
- display a model as installed before its files are downloaded, verified and registered;
- display download progress that is not measured from a real transfer;
- claim Google Drive is synchronized without retrieving and processing actual authorized changes;
- present sample documents as user Drive files;
- claim a sandbox job ran unless an isolated worker actually ran it;
- claim a task is complete before output verification;
- fabricate hardware specifications, model latency, token use, citations or telemetry;
- silently switch private data to a public model;
- use animation to imply an operation is happening when no corresponding operation exists.
Every feature must have truthful states: not configured, disconnected, connecting, connected, degraded, running, queued, failed, cancelled, needs approval, verified, and unknown as appropriate.
0.3 Work continuously and report accurately
Do not stop after writing a plan or creating UI screens. Implement as much as can be safely completed in the current environment. Where an external credential, device, GPU, Google verification, production domain or physical hardware is required, implement the real code path, configuration contract, mocks only in test environments, and explicit setup instructions. Do not pretend the external integration has been live-tested.

For every phase, report:
- files changed;
- functionality implemented;
- tests run and their exact result;
- tests not run and why;
- external prerequisites;
- remaining defects;
- rollback or migration instructions.
