2. MASTER ARCHITECTURE
Implement well-separated services with typed, versioned contracts. The UI must not call providers, databases, Drive, device bridges or sandbox workers directly.
2.1 Major platform services
1. LORIN Core / Conversation Service — identity, conversation, streaming and user-facing response.
2. Intelligence Orchestrator — reasoning workflow, task planning, specialist coordination and routing decisions.
3. Model Gateway — normalized API to local and approved remote LLM providers.
4. Model Registry and Download Manager — model catalog, compatibility, installation, verification and lifecycle.
5. Knowledge Service — ingestion, parsing, indexing, hybrid search, reranking and citations.
6. Memory Service — explicit memory proposals, provenance, retention, editing, export and deletion.
7. Storage Service — local and remote objects, structured records, metadata, backup and restore.
8. Connector Service — Google Drive first, followed by future approved external sources.
9. Task and Automation Service — durable jobs, scheduling, idempotency, cancellation, retry and approvals.
10. Policy and Authorization Service — owner identity, role checks, scope, risk, privacy classification and approval decisions.
11. Sandbox Control Plane — provisions and supervises isolated execution workers.
12. Windows Companion Service — trusted local hardware, runtime and approved device bridge.
13. Android Companion — authenticated, narrowly scoped mobile capabilities.
14. Multimodal Service — speech, image, video/snapshot and contextual input processing.
15. Audit and Observability Service — truthful system health, traces, logs, metrics and security records.
16. 3D Companion Runtime — character rendering, animation, lipsync and adaptive visual modes.
2.2 Architectural rules
- Use a service boundary for each concern; avoid circular dependencies.
- Use typed request/response schemas and runtime validation.
- Use authenticated server-to-server calls.
- Use durable job queues for long-running work.
- Support cancellation, timeouts, retries and idempotency.
- Enforce authorization in backend services, not merely in UI components.
- Keep local and cloud data paths explicit.
- Make offline capability an intentional mode, not an accidental side effect.
- Keep provider adapters replaceable.
- Keep the existing application stack unless audit evidence supports a migration.
- Define API contracts and event schemas before connecting independent modules.
2.3 Core data and event model
Create or reconcile schema for:
- users/owners and authenticated sessions;
- devices and device grants;
- workspaces and projects;
- conversations and messages;
- memory records and proposals;
- knowledge sources, documents, versions, chunks and embeddings;
- model providers, credentials references, model catalog entries, installed models and inference runs;
- task plans, jobs, steps, approvals and execution attempts;
- sandbox environments, resource policies, artifacts and logs;
- connector accounts, OAuth grants, sync cursors and sync runs;
- audit events and security incidents;
- companion preferences, character assets and presentation settings.
Events should include stable IDs, tenant/owner scope, timestamp, actor, correlation ID, schema version and outcome. Do not place secrets or private document content into logs/events unnecessarily.
3. CAPABILITY GROUP 1 — VISUAL COMPANION (8/8)
Objective
Create an original, high-fidelity, expressive 3D LORIN character that feels like a sophisticated digital intelligence rather than a chatbot avatar. The character must be performant, accessible, responsive and synchronized with actual assistant states.
3.1 Original high-fidelity 3D LORIN character
- Establish an original art direction and character design brief before asset production.
- Produce an original high-quality humanoid/hybrid digital companion, not a replica of a copyrighted movie character or actor.
- Define identity, face proportions, silhouette, materials, wardrobe/technology details, color response and visual scale.
- Create a production-ready rigged GLB/glTF asset with documented license and source files.
- Provide appropriate LODs, texture resolutions, compressed textures and mesh optimization.
- Support desktop high quality and mobile/lower-power quality profiles.
- Keep character appearance consistent across modes and devices.
- Ensure the character reads clearly against dark, light and adaptive backgrounds.
3.2 Facial rigging and expression system
- Implement facial blend shapes/morph targets or a validated equivalent rig.
- Support brow, eyelid, eye, cheek, jaw, lip and head micro-movements.
- Define a curated expression library: neutral, attentive, curious, thinking, explaining, concerned, pleased, surprised, focused, apologetic and error/uncertain states.
- Blend expressions smoothly and avoid uncanny snapping.
- Use procedural micro-expressions sparingly and do not imply emotions as factual internal states.
- Allow expression intensity and animation to be reduced for user comfort.
3.3 Realistic body and gesture animation
- Implement rigged skeletal animation for posture, head motion, shoulders, arms and hands where the asset supports it.
- Add idle breathing, blinking, posture transitions, listening pose, speaking gestures, explanation gestures and transition animations.
- Use animation blending and procedural secondary motion without jitter.
- Keep gestures subtle and context-appropriate; no random exaggerated motion.
- Support interruption and state transitions without animation lockups.
3.4 Lip synchronization with speech
- Synchronize jaw, lips and facial motion with actual generated audio.
- Prefer phoneme/viseme timing from the TTS system when available; otherwise use a validated audio-driven method.
- Handle pauses, punctuation, silence, streamed speech chunks and interruption.
- Account for latency so animation does not lead or lag materially.
- Return to neutral naturally after speech stops.
- Do not animate speech while audio is not being played.
- Provide a measurable lipsync QA procedure with sample utterances and timing tolerance.
3.5 Eye tracking and gaze behaviour
- Implement natural gaze targets: user/camera, interface, visual object, speaking/listening focus and rest position.
- Use smoothing, natural saccades and blink timing.
- Support camera availability and privacy settings; do not claim gaze tracking of the user without actual supported hardware and explicit consent.
- Distinguish character eye direction from actual user tracking.
- Respect reduced motion and accessibility modes.
3.6 Adaptive lighting and environments
- Design coherent visual environments for Companion, Immersive, Compact, Workspace and Ambient modes.
- Use lighting that responds to real states such as listening, speaking, working, alert, offline and error.
- Keep glows restrained and preserve text contrast.
- Adapt the environment to the active task without distracting from content.
- Provide light/dark system compatibility where relevant and maintain LORIN's charcoal/teal/lavender identity.
3.7 Companion, Immersive, Compact and Ambient modes
- Companion: character plus conversation and contextual controls.
- Immersive: full-screen character/environment with interaction controls and optional panels.
- Compact: minimal persistent presence that does not obstruct work.
- Workspace: companion integrated with project/code/research tools.
- Ambient: low-distraction state with restrained status and notifications.
- Preserve conversation state and task state when switching modes.
- Support responsive layouts and keyboard/touch navigation.
- Save user presentation preferences safely.
3.8 GPU quality scaling and fallback
- Detect rendering capabilities safely and select an appropriate quality tier.
- Prefer WebGPU where supported and stable; provide WebGL fallback.
- Use low/medium/high/ultra profiles based on measured capability, not guessed device labels.
- Implement LOD, texture compression, frame-rate-aware quality adjustment, memory monitoring and graceful context-loss recovery.
- Support a 2D/static companion fallback for unsupported devices.
- Respect battery and thermal constraints where observable.
- Never make the entire application unusable if 3D rendering fails.
Visual companion acceptance tests
- Character loads from a real asset and displays in all supported modes.
- WebGPU and WebGL fallback both work on supported test devices.
- Facial expressions and body animations transition without visible snapping.
- TTS audio and visemes are synchronized within a defined tested tolerance.
- Interrupting speech stops audio and lip motion promptly.
- GPU/context failure returns to a usable fallback without crashing conversation.
- Reduced-motion and keyboard-accessibility settings work.
- No animation is used to fake model inference or task execution.
4. CAPABILITY GROUP 2 — CORE AI INTELLIGENCE (9/9)
Objective
Build LORIN's own private AI runtime and a unified gateway connecting it to approved public LLMs. LORIN owns routing, privacy, knowledge, tools, memory, evaluation and orchestration; providers are replaceable services.
4.1 Private LORIN model infrastructure
- Support local inference through Ollama as the initial Windows-friendly runtime.
- Design a provider-neutral inference adapter so suitable future runtimes, including vLLM, can be added.
- Keep model weights under owner-controlled storage.
- Separate chat LLMs, vision models, embedding models, rerankers, speech models and specialist models.
- Support local-only operation when a compatible model is installed.
- Do not claim a model is private simply because an API is local; inspect network routing and logs.
- Provide model licenses, source, version, quantization and provenance.
4.2 Gemini and additional approved model providers
- Implement a genuine Gemini provider adapter with server-side secret handling.
- Provide a generic provider contract for future approved providers.
- Store secret references in a secure backend secret manager or OS credential store; never expose API keys to browser bundles.
- Support provider enable/disable, health, quotas, model lists and capability metadata.
- Distinguish configured, authenticated, healthy, degraded and unavailable states.
- Verify connectivity with actual authenticated calls and safe health checks.
4.3 Unified Model Gateway
- Create a single normalized API for chat completion, streaming, embeddings, reranking and supported structured/tool outputs.
- Define provider capability metadata and compatibility checks.
- Normalize errors without hiding provider-specific diagnostics.
- Support request IDs, cancellation, timeouts, bounded retries and rate limiting.
- Attribute every response to the actual provider/model used.
- Track actual latency, token usage and cost only where provider data supports it.
- Keep UI components independent of provider-specific APIs.
4.4 Automatic and manual model routing
- Implement manual model selection and automatic task-based routing.
- Provide privacy modes: local-only, local-first and cloud-assisted.
- Classify data before routing: public, internal, personal, sensitive and strictly local.
- Respect project and Private Vault boundaries.
- Route using task type, model capabilities, health, context size, performance and owner policy.
- Never silently send private data to a cloud provider when local inference fails.
- Require explicit permission when a request would move data to a less-private route.
- Show the actual routing decision and reason in appropriate diagnostics.
4.5 Real streaming and interruption
- Implement server-side streaming through the gateway to the UI.
- Support partial text, tool events, status events and completion/error signals.
- Implement stop/cancel for user interruption and task cancellation.
- Stop upstream generation when possible rather than merely hiding output.
- Handle connection drops, reconnects and partial responses without duplicating content.
- Preserve conversation integrity and mark incomplete/failed generations honestly.
4.6 Model downloads and installation
- Implement a model catalog with publisher, license, source, revision, size, architecture, quantization, context window, capabilities and hardware requirements.
- Support actual downloads or runtime-native pulls, pause/resume where supported, cancellation, retry and cleanup.
- Verify file integrity using trusted checksums/signatures when available.
- Register a model only after successful verification and runtime compatibility checks.
- Support model storage paths, disk usage, updates, removal and rollback to prior versions where feasible.
- Do not attempt unsafe arbitrary model-file execution.
4.7 Hardware detection and compatibility
- Build a trusted Windows companion probe for CPU, RAM, GPU, VRAM, available disk, operating system, drivers and runtime capability.
- Report exact source and timestamp of hardware readings.
- Browser-only data must not be presented as full hardware telemetry.
- Estimate model compatibility conservatively and show uncertainty.
- Warn before large downloads and check available disk space.
- Never automatically download a model that exceeds configured resource limits.
4.8 Embeddings and reranking
- Select and document embedding models and dimensions.
- Keep embedding model/version metadata with each indexed chunk.
- Support re-embedding migrations safely.
- Implement hybrid lexical and vector retrieval.
- Add reranking as an optional measured second stage.
- Respect tenant/project/document permissions before and after retrieval.
- Evaluate precision, recall and ranking quality against a representative test corpus.
4.9 Model evaluation and controlled fine-tuning
- Build evaluation datasets for factuality, instruction following, code tasks, tool use, retrieval, privacy and refusal behaviour.
- Benchmark models on the target hardware and representative tasks.
- Record versioned scores and regressions.
- Fine-tuning is a later, owner-approved workflow using curated datasets, provenance, license review and reproducible configuration.
- Prefer parameter-efficient approaches such as LoRA where appropriate.
- Never automatically train on every conversation or every newly imported file.
- Require evaluation before activating a fine-tuned model; support rollback.
AI acceptance tests
- Local inference succeeds with external network disabled when a supported local model is installed.
- Gemini works only with a valid authorized credential and reports invalid credentials accurately.
- Local-only mode never calls a public provider.
- Cloud fallback never occurs silently.
- Streaming produces incremental output and cancellation stops the real request.
- Model downloads show measured progress and verify integrity.
- An uninstalled model cannot be selected as available for inference.
- Hardware readings come from the trusted companion and are timestamped.
- Embedding dimensions and model versions are validated.
- Evaluation results are reproducible and fine-tuned model activation is reversible.
5. CAPABILITY GROUP 3 — KNOWLEDGE AND MEMORY (8/8)
Objective
Give LORIN a persistent, searchable, permission-aware Knowledge Vault and memory system spanning local storage, approved remote storage and connected sources. Knowledge updates must not require retraining model weights.
5.1 Google Drive OAuth and file selection
- Create a dedicated Google Cloud project and configure the Drive API, OAuth consent and exact redirect URLs.
- Implement secure OAuth state/CSRF validation and backend token exchange.
- Use Google Picker or supported selection UX to select files/folders.
- Prefer the narrowest viable scope, beginning with drive.file where the workflow permits.
- Explain when broader scopes are needed and their verification/security implications.
- Store refresh tokens encrypted on the backend or protected credential store; never in localStorage, sessionStorage or frontend JS.
- Support token expiry, refresh, revocation and disconnect.
- Show the actual account and authorized scope.
5.2 Automatic and recoverable synchronization
- Implement initial indexing and incremental synchronization using Drive Changes API where supported.
- Store durable page tokens/cursors and sync checkpoints.
- Treat webhook/watch notifications as prompts to retrieve changes, not proof that sync is complete.
- Process create, update, rename, move, delete and permission-change events.
- Implement periodic reconciliation, retries, backoff and restart recovery.
- Use stable source IDs, version metadata and content hashes to avoid duplicate imports.
- Handle Google Docs, Sheets and Slides through supported export methods.
- Show last successful sync, queue, errors, retry and pause state.
- Propagate deletion/revocation to the LORIN index according to retention policy.
5.3 Persistent local and remote Knowledge Vault
- Keep an owner-controlled local vault for offline capability.
- Support approved private remote storage for synchronization and availability across devices.
- Separate file/object storage from structured metadata and vector indexes.
- Encrypt sensitive material at rest and in transit.
- Support file types including PDF, DOCX, TXT, Markdown, CSV, JSON, HTML, images where supported, and Google Workspace exports.
- Store source, owner, project, file version, content hash, parser version, sensitivity, timestamps and processing state.
- Keep raw source and extracted/indexed derivatives traceable.
5.4 Hybrid keyword and vector search
- Implement lexical search, vector similarity and optional reranking.
- Support filters by project, source, file type, time, owner and sensitivity.
- Chunk documents by structure where possible rather than arbitrary character slicing.
- Preserve page/slide/sheet/section offsets for citations.
- Apply permission checks before returning results and before context enters an LLM request.
- Evaluate retrieval quality on a realistic test corpus.
5.5 Accurate source citations
- Every retrieved excerpt should carry source ID, title, location, version and content span.
- The answer must cite the actual source passages used.
- Do not fabricate page numbers, file names, quotes or source links.
- Distinguish user knowledge sources from external web sources.
- If retrieval finds insufficient evidence, say so rather than inventing an answer.
- Preserve citations through summarization and generated reports.
5.6 Memory provenance and owner controls
- Separate episodic conversation history, semantic memories, preferences, project decisions and task history.
- Use a memory proposal/approval policy for durable personal information.
- Record provenance: source conversation/document, date, owner approval, confidence and update history.
- Provide inspect, search, edit, pin, expire, export and delete controls.
- Support “do not remember” and per-project memory settings.
- Do not automatically convert every conversation into permanent memory.
- Explain the difference between searchable knowledge and model training.
5.7 Project-specific context and isolation
- Scope project memories, documents, conversations and indexes by owner and project.
- Do not leak one project's private data into another project or public/shared workspace.
- Require explicit cross-project sharing grants.
- Add tests for cross-tenant and cross-project leakage.
- Allow project archival and export without losing provenance.
5.8 Export, deletion and backup recovery
- Export user data in documented open formats.
- Support selective and full deletion with propagation to indexes, caches and replicas according to policy.
- Design retention windows and legal/operational constraints transparently.
- Back up databases, metadata, object stores and critical configuration separately.
- Encrypt backups and manage keys safely.
- Test actual restore to a clean environment.
- Provide backup status based on real completed jobs only.
Knowledge and memory acceptance tests
- Connect a real Google account and select an authorized test file.
- Import, index, search and cite the real content.
- Edit the source and verify the index updates.
- Rename/move/delete/revoke access and verify correct state propagation.
- Stop the service mid-sync and verify safe recovery without duplication.
- Attempt cross-project retrieval and confirm it is blocked.
- Save, inspect, edit, export and delete a memory with provenance.
- Restore a backup to a clean test environment and verify integrity.
6. CAPABILITY GROUP 4 — SANDBOX AND EXECUTION (8/8)
Objective
Allow LORIN to perform real digital work—especially software engineering and data tasks—inside isolated, temporary, policy-controlled environments without granting unrestricted access to the owner's device or production systems.
6.1 Isolated Docker or microVM workers
- Build a sandbox control plane separate from the main application.
- Use rootless Docker with hardened configuration for appropriate workloads.
- Use stronger microVM isolation for higher-risk/untrusted code when available.
- Never expose the Docker socket to a worker or model.
- Run non-root users, drop unnecessary capabilities and apply seccomp/AppArmor or equivalent controls.
- Use immutable base images and pinned dependency sources where practical.
- Provision a distinct environment per job or trust boundary.
- Automatically destroy or quarantine completed/failed environments according to retention policy.
6.2 Code execution and development environments
- Support approved languages and project types based on installed images/toolchains.
- Create a snapshot/branch/worktree instead of modifying originals directly.
- Provide controlled package installation, command execution, test, lint, typecheck and build operations.
- Capture stdout, stderr, exit codes and duration.
- Support dev servers and previews only within network boundaries.
- Prevent arbitrary commands from escaping the worker.
- Use workspace manifests and explicit file mounts.
6.3 Browser research and testing sandbox
- Provide isolated browser workers for approved browsing, application testing and screenshot capture.
- Keep browser cookies, passwords and owner's personal browser profile inaccessible.
- Use separate temporary identities and sessions.
- Restrict navigation and downloads according to job policy.
- Treat web pages as untrusted content and defend against prompt injection.
- Require approval for authenticated account actions, purchases, posting, messaging or destructive operations.
6.4 Terminal and live job monitoring
- Provide a real terminal/command execution stream connected to the isolated worker.
- Display job ID, owner, project, command policy, start time, elapsed time, resource consumption and state.
- Stream logs with redaction and bounded retention.
- Support cancel/terminate and ensure worker teardown.
- Distinguish queued, provisioning, running, verifying, succeeded, failed, cancelled and timed-out states.
- Never show fabricated process output or fake logs.
6.5 Resource and network restrictions
- Enforce CPU, memory, GPU, disk, process count and execution-time limits.
- Apply default-deny outbound network policy.
- Permit only explicitly approved domains/services and required package registries.
- Use egress proxying and DNS restrictions where appropriate.
- Apply download size and archive extraction limits.
- Prevent fork bombs, zip bombs, resource exhaustion and unbounded output.
- Terminate workers exceeding limits and retain a security event.
6.6 Secure project workspaces
- Require explicit owner/project grant for each workspace.
- Mount only required files and paths.
- Default input mounts to read-only.
- Write into a temporary working copy or branch.
- Keep secrets out of the worker; inject narrowly scoped short-lived credentials only when necessary and authorized.
- Prevent path traversal and symlink escape.
- Separate user projects and job outputs.
6.7 File diffs and artifact verification
- Produce structured file manifests and diffs.
- Scan generated/downloaded artifacts.
- Validate file paths, sizes, types and checksums.
- Run relevant tests against the working copy.
- Show additions, modifications, deletions and generated artifacts.
- Keep original project unchanged until approved delivery.
- Provide rollback/revert information.
6.8 Approval, delivery and rollback
- Use risk-based approvals for external, destructive, production and sensitive actions.
- Bind an approval to exact action, arguments, target, artifact hash and expiry.
- Revalidate user identity and policy immediately before execution.
- Never treat model output as approval.
- Deliver only approved artifacts to approved destinations.
- Use atomic writes or versioned deployment where possible.
- Keep rollback points and verify restoration.
Sandbox acceptance tests
- Run a harmless test program in an isolated worker and verify output/exit code.
- Confirm no host filesystem access beyond explicit mounts.
- Confirm no Docker socket, browser profile or secret access.
- Confirm outbound network is blocked unless explicitly permitted.
- Trigger CPU/memory/time limits and verify termination.
- Run malicious path/symlink/archive tests and confirm containment.
- Cancel a job and verify worker teardown.
- Generate file diffs, approve delivery and verify original changes only after approval.
- Roll back delivered changes and verify integrity.
