- Use Windows Credential Manager/DPAPI or an equivalent OS-protected mechanism for secrets.
- Apply restrictive ACLs, signed updates, least privilege and service hardening.
- Provide start/stop, logs, diagnostic bundle, update, rollback and uninstall.
- Make offline local inference possible when configured.
8.2 Secure Android integration
- Build a native Android companion or a documented native integration layer.
- Use Android permission APIs correctly and request only capabilities required for the current feature.
- Provide device registration, authentication, revocation and status.
- Support notifications and approved local actions within Android platform rules.
- Keep background behavior visible and compliant with platform restrictions.
- Do not promise capabilities that Android permissions or vendor APIs do not support.
8.3 Device pairing and revocation
- Use cryptographically strong one-time pairing codes/tokens.
- Bind grants to owner, device identity, capability and expiry.
- Support rotation, revocation and lost-device procedures.
- Require reauthentication for sensitive pairing changes.
- Record enrollment and revocation events.
- Prevent device identity spoofing and replay.
8.4 Approved filesystem and application access
- Expose only explicitly selected directories and app capabilities.
- Prefer read-only access initially.
- Use scoped operations rather than a general-purpose remote shell.
- Apply path canonicalization, ACL checks and audit logging.
- Protect browser profiles, password stores, private keys and unrelated personal files.
- Require separate approval for writes, deletes, launches and external submissions.
8.5 Connected services and device status
- Create a connector registry with capability descriptions, authentication state, last successful check and error state.
- Show actual connection health and timestamp.
- Support token revocation and connection removal.
- Integrate calendar/email/cloud services only through approved APIs and scopes.
- Keep service secrets out of frontend code.
8.6 Smart-home integrations
- Add supported integrations through documented local/cloud APIs (for example, a controlled Home Assistant connector where configured).
- Discover devices only within authorized networks/accounts.
- Map capabilities and safety risk per device.
- Require confirmation for security-relevant, destructive or safety-sensitive actions.
- Keep high-impact physical actions disabled by default.
- Log the command, target, authorization and observed result.
8.7 Permission-controlled remote actions
- Define capability grants per device and action.
- Use short-lived delegated tokens and server-side policy checks.
- Require step-up authentication or approval for high-risk actions.
- Never allow an LLM to mint credentials or expand its own scope.
- Add rate limits and replay protection.
- Fail closed when device status or authorization is uncertain.
8.8 Cross-device task continuity
- Synchronize approved task state, conversation summaries and project context across enrolled devices.
- Keep local-only data on the local device unless the owner explicitly changes policy.
- Handle offline edits and conflicts with versioning.
- Avoid duplicate task execution with idempotency keys.
- Allow remote device sign-out/revocation.
- Display the source device and current task owner/state.
Connected environment acceptance tests
- Pair a real Windows device and verify its identity.
- Revoke it and confirm all grants stop working.
- Verify filesystem access is restricted to approved paths.
- Confirm secrets are stored using OS-protected storage.
- Test Android permission denial and revocation.
- Execute an approved low-risk device action and verify the real result.
- Confirm high-risk actions require additional authorization.
- Disconnect a device and verify queued tasks do not execute twice on reconnect.
9. CAPABILITY GROUP 7 — AUTONOMY AND GIDEON (8/8)
Objective
Give LORIN continuity, planning, task execution and useful initiative while keeping the owner in control. LORIN may reason and prepare actions autonomously inside its grants; it may not grant itself new authority.
9.1 Persistent decision history
- Store meaningful decisions, project milestones, rationale, outcomes and source references.
- Distinguish facts, assumptions, proposals and decisions.
- Record who approved a decision and when.
- Support timeline, search, filtering and correction.
- Preserve superseded decisions with links to the replacement rather than silently overwriting history.
9.2 Long-term planning and project continuity
- Maintain owner-approved project goals, milestones, dependencies, risks and next actions.
- Track project status from actual task results.
- Summarize context when resuming a project.
- Identify stale or conflicting plans.
- Ask for clarification only when essential; otherwise make a safe, explicit assumption and label it.
- Support cross-session and cross-device continuity with access controls.
9.3 Multi-step task orchestration
- Convert user intent into structured plans with steps, dependencies, resources, risk and acceptance criteria.
- Separate planning from execution.
- Track task state durably.
- Support cancellation, pause/resume and failure recovery.
- Use idempotency to prevent duplicate side effects.
- Re-plan when real results differ from expectations.
- Report completed, failed, blocked and unverified steps separately.
9.4 Parallel internal specialist execution
- Allow Owen to coordinate parallel Iris/Nolan/Riley/GIDEON/Lena work where dependencies permit.
- Give each specialist a scoped task and minimum required context.
- Use shared policy and authorization; specialists cannot bypass Lena Guardian.
- Validate specialist outputs before synthesis.
- Detect contradictions and provenance gaps.
- Prevent parallel workers from performing conflicting writes or duplicate external actions.
- LORIN remains the single user-facing interface.
9.5 Scheduled tasks and recovery
- Implement durable schedules with owner timezone and clear recurrence rules.
- Support missed-run policy, retry bounds, deduplication and cancellation.
- Store execution history and next run.
- Pause schedules when credentials are revoked or permissions change.
- Require approval for schedules that perform external side effects.
- Do not claim a schedule is running unless the scheduler confirms it.
9.6 Proactive recommendations with consent
- Offer useful suggestions based on approved projects, deadlines and preferences.
- Let the owner configure what signals can be used and how often suggestions appear.
- Avoid intrusive or excessive notifications.
- Explain why a recommendation was made.
- Never perform consequential external actions solely because a proactive model suggested them.
- Provide pause/disable controls.
9.7 Continuous evaluation and improvement proposals
- Monitor real task outcomes, errors, latency, retrieval quality and user corrections where permitted.
- Identify patterns and propose improvements.
- Use regression datasets and controlled experiments.
- Require approval for code changes, configuration changes, model changes and training.
- Keep a known-good version and rollback path.
- Never let LORIN rewrite its own security policy or remove owner controls.
- Do not train automatically on private conversations.
9.8 Transparent action and memory audit trail
- Record the initiating user request, plan, policy decision, approval, tool, arguments (redacted where sensitive), execution result and verification.
- Record memory creation/update/deletion provenance.
- Make history searchable and exportable.
- Protect audit records from ordinary agent modification.
- Clearly show pending approval, running, succeeded, failed, denied and rolled-back outcomes.
- Never rewrite a failed record as success.
Autonomy acceptance tests
- Resume a multi-step project from saved approved context.
- Run dependent and independent task steps with correct ordering.
- Test a specialist conflict and verify LORIN detects/reconciles it.
- Retry a failed idempotent operation without duplicate side effects.
- Verify scheduled task pause/revoke behavior.
- Confirm proactive suggestions do not trigger external actions without authorization.
- Verify audit records for approvals, execution, failure and rollback.
- Confirm agents cannot change their own permissions or security policies.
10. CAPABILITY GROUP 8 — SECURITY AND PRODUCTION (9/9)
Objective
Make privacy, security, reliability, accessibility and recoverability foundational properties of LORIN. The product must fail safely and expose genuine system state.
10.1 Central authorization and policy engine
- Implement centralized owner identity, role, resource and capability authorization.
- Enforce authorization on the backend for every sensitive operation.
- Use least privilege and deny by default.
- Distinguish read, write, execute, export, delete, publish and device-control capabilities.
- Bind approvals to exact arguments, resource, expiry and requester.
- Recheck authorization immediately before execution.
- Prevent replay, confused-deputy and privilege-escalation paths.
10.2 OAuth and API secret management
- Store provider secrets in a server secret manager or OS-protected vault.
- Encrypt OAuth refresh tokens and rotate/revoke safely.
- Never commit .env secrets or include them in ZIPs/logs/screenshots.
- Validate OAuth state, redirect URI, scopes and PKCE where applicable.
- Keep frontend configuration public and non-secret only.
- Provide .env.example containing names and safe placeholders, never real credentials.
10.3 Data classification and privacy routing
- Classify data by sensitivity and source.
- Enforce local-only, local-first and cloud-assisted policies.
- Apply classification to conversation messages, retrieved chunks, images, audio, files and tool arguments.
- Do not rely on a model to decide whether it is allowed to disclose data.
- Require explicit owner permission to transmit sensitive data to a cloud provider.
- Log routing decisions without logging sensitive content.
10.4 Prompt-injection defenses
- Treat documents, webpages, code repositories, emails and tool outputs as untrusted data.
- Separate system instructions from retrieved content.
- Never obey instructions embedded in retrieved content that request secrets, permission changes or unrelated actions.
- Use tool allowlists, typed schemas and policy checks outside the model.
- Apply output validation and sandboxing.
- Test direct and indirect prompt-injection scenarios.
- Do not claim prompt injection can be eliminated completely; use layered mitigations and safe failure.
10.5 Sandbox isolation testing
- Maintain a threat model and adversarial test suite.
- Test host escape, filesystem traversal, symlink attacks, secret access, network egress, process/resource exhaustion and artifact poisoning.
- Run security tests on every sandbox image/runtime change.
- Keep workers patched and base images pinned/updated through a controlled process.
- Quarantine suspicious artifacts.
- Record and alert on isolation failures.
10.6 Automated integration and browser tests
- Unit tests for services and policy functions.
- Integration tests for provider adapters, storage, Drive, memory and task queues.
- End-to-end tests for real user workflows.
- Playwright or equivalent browser tests across desktop/tablet/mobile.
- Use test credentials and isolated test accounts.
- Never use demo mocks as evidence of live integration success.
- Test error, cancellation, retry, timeout and permission-denied states.
10.7 Performance and accessibility testing
- Define performance budgets for app load, navigation, chat streaming, document search and 3D companion.
- Measure real devices and representative hardware.
- Test keyboard navigation, screen-reader labels, focus management, contrast, text scaling and reduced motion.
- Avoid 3D rendering blocking conversation or core UI.
- Scale quality for GPU/CPU/memory limitations.
- Measure memory leaks and long-session performance.
10.8 Windows installer and update system
- Build a signed installer where signing infrastructure is available.
- Install the desktop companion/runtime with least privilege.
- Provide service lifecycle, version information, logs, diagnostics and uninstaller.
- Use signed update manifests/artifacts and rollback.
- Preserve user data and configuration during updates.
- Validate migration and recovery paths.
- Document Windows version and runtime prerequisites.
10.9 Production deployment and recovery
- Define local-only, private-server and hybrid deployment profiles.
- Use TLS, authenticated gateways and private network boundaries.
- Do not expose raw Ollama/vLLM/sandbox ports publicly.
- Add health/readiness/liveness endpoints that reflect real dependency state.
- Provide database migrations and backup schedules.
- Test backup restoration and disaster recovery.
- Use monitoring with secret/data redaction.
- Provide rollback and incident response procedures.
- Document domain, certificate, firewall, DNS and service configuration.
- Do not label production-ready until all release gates pass.
Security and production acceptance tests
- Verify authorization at every API/service boundary.
- Attempt access with expired, revoked and wrong-owner tokens.
- Verify secrets do not occur in bundles, logs, test screenshots or archives.
- Run prompt-injection and data-exfiltration tests.
- Run sandbox adversarial tests.
- Complete desktop/tablet/mobile browser regression.
- Complete accessibility and performance checks.
- Install/update/uninstall the Windows companion in a clean VM.
- Restore database and object storage into a clean environment.
- Verify production ports and gateways are not publicly exposing internal model or sandbox services.
